WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.630
I'm David. I run R
for the rest of us.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.380
And I'm here to talk
to you about starting

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:14.330
a business with the R.
But here's the thing.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:17.050
I never actually intended
to start my own business

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.900
and to show you how
unintentional my path to doing

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:21.730
what I do now has been.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.980
Let me give you a brief story.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:28.420
In 2015, I was working
my last 9 to 5 job.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.410
My wife had just started
a job at the time.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:33.790
It was a pretty challenging job,
but she likes challenging jobs.

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:36.580
At the time we were
hoping to have kids,

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.890
so we were overjoyed
when we found out

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:41.740
that she was pregnant
with not one but two kids,

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.990
we were going to have twins.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:47.000
Now, at the time we
talked about what to do.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:47.880
I really like kids.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.630
I used to be an
elementary school teacher,

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:52.510
so we decided that I would
stay home with our kids

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.140
and she would continue working.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.260
Now, for those of
you who have kids,

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.050
you might not be surprised
to hear that my pre-kid

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:05.040
vision of what being a full time
stay at home dad would be like

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.770
was pretty different from
the post-kid reality.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:12.270
And so after a few months, my
wife very kindly said to me,

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:15.330
you know, it's OK if this
isn't going to quite work out

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.640
the way we imagined it might.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.530
What happened, though,
at the same time

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.240
was that the challenging
job that my wife

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.990
Rachel had started actually
came to feel untenable.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:29.200
Having young kids and an
incredibly challenging job

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.750
was just a bit too much, and
she ended up leaving that job.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:37.240
So in a few months we had gone
from having two jobs and 0 kids

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.052
to having 0
jobs and two kids.

00:01:41.052 --> 00:01:42.760
And it was at that
time I realized, OK, I

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:46.250
need to do something to bring in
a little income for our family.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:50.170
So I started doing some
freelance data analysis work.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:51.980
I was an Excel user at the time.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:55.120
I used Excel for everything,
but I got a little bit bored

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.860
and I had heard
about R and I had

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:00.550
heard that R could do some
nice stuff, automate things,

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:01.960
make nice dataviz.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.210
And I was like, OK,
cool, let me learn that.

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:05.390
I taught myself R

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:08.199
and in the process
realized, first of all,

00:02:08.199 --> 00:02:09.323
that I really liked it.

00:02:09.323 --> 00:02:11.740
And second of all, that there
were a lot of people like me

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.400
who also wanted to learn

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.720
R and so it was from that
experience that I started

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.550
R for the Rest of Us. Today,
R for the Rest of Us

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:24.380
does both education work,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.580
We have online courses,
trainings for organizations,

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.560
as well as consulting work.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.160
And I'm going to use
examples from our business,

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:37.070
but this isn't really ultimately
a talk about my business.

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:40.980
This is a talk
about your business.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.830
I'm going to focus
mostly on marketing,

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.490
on how to sell your work.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:47.138
And the reason why
is it's a thing

00:02:47.138 --> 00:02:48.930
that people who are
thinking about starting

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.820
their own business
ask me the most.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.010
They say, "David, I want
to start my own business,

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.200
"but I have no idea
how to find clients.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.550
How did you do it?"

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:01.097
And I know there are some
people potentially in the room.

00:03:01.097 --> 00:03:03.430
Maybe you're waiting for the
next talk who are thinking,

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:04.930
"You know what, I
don't actually want

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.840
to start my own business,"
before you run for the doors.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.150
I think the reason you
might consider staying

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:13.840
is that marketing matters,
even if you're just working.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.880
Even if you are working
for someone else.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.190
So you need to, for
example, sell your own work

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:19.730
within your own organization

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:22.615
so your boss or your coworkers
see what you're capable of.

00:03:22.615 --> 00:03:24.490
Maybe you're making an
R package and you want

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:25.860
to get it out into the world.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:27.220
You need to market it.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:30.040
So rather than put
our heads in the sand

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:36.430
and not do any marketing, let's
learn how to do marketing well.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:37.870
A quick question.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:41.170
If you would say that you like
marketing, would you

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:42.390
go ahead and raise your hand?

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:53.100
So I see a handful,
maybe like 10 max.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:56.520
Not that many people in the
room, I would say, overall

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:58.030
say they enjoy marketing.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:00.090
And I think the
reason why is this.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.640
Technical people
like us tend to be

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.130
pretty allergic to marketing.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:09.330
And I know that because I in the
past very much felt like this.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:12.022
We think, OK, if I write
really good code, that

00:04:12.022 --> 00:04:12.730
should be enough.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:14.680
I shouldn't have to
go out and sell it.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.269
Right? What I've
realized is that

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:21.630
another thing I've come to
realize in the last few years

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:24.780
is that when people say that
they don't like marketing,

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.970
the marketing that
they're thinking of

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:28.930
is often kind of
like this, right?

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:31.110
Like think about stuff
you've seen on social media

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:35.490
with a ton of hashtags linked to
who knows where trying to sell

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:36.700
who knows what, right?

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.160
Like that type of
sleazy marketing.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.790
But what I've come to realize
in running R for the Rest of Us

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.560
is that marketing can
actually look more

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.260
like this, can look
like education,

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.920
it can look like teaching.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.770
And so I think when
you do marketing well,

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:51.600
it doesn't actually
even necessarily

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.210
seem like marketing.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.700
Let me give some examples
from other people who

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.000
run kind of consulting
businesses of how they talk

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.600
about marketing and ways
that their approach also

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.540
shows up in what we do
with R for the Rest of Us.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:11.590
So Paul Minors was asked
how he does marketing,

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:12.790
how he finds his clients.

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:15.980
He's a consultant who trains
on Asana and Pipedrive.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:18.280
The details don't really
matter, but what he said

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:20.920
is, "The way I do
marketing is simple.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:24.410
"I go to where my
ideal clients are.

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:26.420
And I'm hopeful."

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:29.750
And so with R for the Rest
of Us, what that's meant

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:32.772
is we go to where people
are, which, probably

00:05:32.772 --> 00:05:34.230
won't be a huge
surprise for folks

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:35.790
here, is Twitter.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.600
That's where the R
community tends to show up.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:40.860
And the way we're
helpful is every day

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:42.530
For the last 3 and
1/2 years, we have

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:47.900
tweeted out one tip
every day at 7:30 AM.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.480
Sometimes things that we
create, oftentimes things

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.460
that other people create.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.540
And so through doing that.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:56.000
We've been
able to build up

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.940
a following of over
15,000 followers, which

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:00.570
becomes useful in ways

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.350
I'll talk about shortly.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:06.810
Wes Bos talks about
his approach being this.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:12.720
"Share your knowledge for free
with no strings attached."

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:14.470
On the R for the
Rest of Us website

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:15.710
we have a blog.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:17.670
There are a bunch of posts.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:22.760
This is an example of one
on how to set up Git and GitHub

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:24.870
if you're working with
R, and I wrote it first

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:26.912
of all because it was
something I struggled with.

00:06:26.912 --> 00:06:29.582
So I figured other people
probably would as well.

00:06:29.582 --> 00:06:31.290
People can go on the
R for the Rest of Us

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.310
website, can read this for free.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:36.600
They don't have to
pay us anything.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:38.800
And I'm totally fine with that.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:41.350
I want to give that value.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:44.620
And the thing with
offering things for free

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:48.190
is that, this is taken
from Adam Wathan, who

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:50.110
says that "Giving away
your work for free

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:52.760
"is like compressing a spring.

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:55.040
"And that spring releases
when you finally

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.710
have something up for sale."

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:01.770
So rather than thinking
about marketing is going out

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:04.560
and saying, "Hey, I'm selling
this, I'm selling that."

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.530
It's much better if you
can go out and offer value,

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:11.010
offer things for free, and
once you get that goodwill,

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:12.810
hopefully get some
following as well,

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.530
then you can offer
things that are paid.

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:19.020
So this is an email that
I send out twice a year

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:20.730
announcing the start
of a program called

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:24.960
R in 3 months, where we do
a training thing, a three month

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:27.570
training program for folks.

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:29.670
And when I send that out,
because we've built up

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:32.160
that goodwill, because we've
offered things for free,

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:36.050
people are more
interested in signing up.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:38.540
I'm going to now offer
kind of four lessons that

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:41.120
have been really useful
for me in thinking

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:43.820
about how to find clients.

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:47.000
So if we think about OK
marketing done well generally

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:49.310
looks kind of like education.

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:55.410
What are some more specific ways
that we can look for clients?

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:58.050
So the first lesson
that I'm going to offer

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:01.970
is to think about going deep,

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:03.560
not broad.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:07.130
So people will sometimes
say to me, "Hey,

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:08.550
"I really like what you're doing.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:10.520
"You know, I
like teaching, too.

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:13.040
"Like, I'd love to start
a business training

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:15.630
people to learn R."

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:17.650
and they'll say--and
I'll ask, "OK great.

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:20.580
"Like, who are you thinking about
trying to work with?" and they say,

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:22.460
"Well, anybody."

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:27.680
But here's the problem if
you try to speak to everyone.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:28.950
No one will listen.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:32.840
Your message is just too
general and people don't--it's

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:35.690
not going to resonate
if it's overly general.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:38.000
So instead, what you
really want to do

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.190
is find your people.
In business speak,

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:43.340
people will talk about
niching down, finding

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:46.760
a niche that you can work in.

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:49.340
And so with R for
the Rest of Us, even

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:54.080
the name of the business is
that was like my first attempt

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.090
to kind of niche down.

00:08:56.090 --> 00:08:59.790
I'm not necessarily
trying to train everybody.

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:01.282
I'm training the rest of us.

00:09:01.282 --> 00:09:02.990
Now, of course,
everybody interprets that

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:04.170
slightly differently.

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:07.460
But generally speaking, what I
think of as our ideal audience

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:10.910
is people who want to learn
R but are intimidated by it.

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:16.120
And I pitch everything to
that specific audience.

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:21.730
On marketing emails that go out,
in the footer of those emails,

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.480
This is what the text says.

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:25.880
Think you know what
an R user looks like?

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:28.150
I'm probably not what
you have in mind.

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:29.680
I'm not a hardcore quant.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:32.350
I won't jump on you
for any small mistake

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:34.660
and I want to help
others avoid the pain.

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:36.670
And you can see
through this language

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:41.230
I am trying to identify
this is the type of person

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:43.795
that I think R for the
Rest of Us is best for.

00:09:43.795 --> 00:09:45.670
It's not that other
people couldn't--like any,

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:47.980
I think anybody can learn
R through the materials

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:48.950
that we offer.

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:51.380
But this is really like
our ideal audience.

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:53.810
And so for some people
that's not going to resonate

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:54.620
and that's OK.

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:56.890
But for other people
that really will resonate

00:09:56.890 --> 00:10:00.940
and they'll be inclined to
work to learn R through us.

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:03.400
Whereas a more general
framing probably

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:06.240
wouldn't appeal to them.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:08.820
In addition to turning
people on, having language

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:12.450
that speaks to certain
people, it's really important

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:13.825
also to turn people off.

00:10:13.825 --> 00:10:15.450
And I know that sounds
counterintuitive

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.950
because you think, well, you
want to have an offering that's

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:21.390
going to be like
applicable to anybody,

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:23.850
but it actually
doesn't work very well.

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:26.460
And turning people off is
a really effective strategy

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:31.360
to help you identify the niche
that you do want to work in.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:33.530
So I'm in the process
of writing a book.

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:36.280
It's called R
without statistics,

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:40.630
which is my attempt to both
say what the book is, but also

00:10:40.630 --> 00:10:41.820
what it's not.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:45.640
It's, broadly speaking, a
book about quirky, interesting

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:47.920
things that you can
do with R that don't

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.750
involve any complex statistics.

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:54.880
So when I tweeted about this
in march, I got this feedback.

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:57.320
Luis wrote "Marketing tip.

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:59.140
"The label will
create a huge barrier

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:01.810
among statisticians
and scholars."

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:04.730
And I put this up not in
any way to disparage Luis.

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:07.090
Like, I really
appreciate the feedback,

00:11:07.090 --> 00:11:10.560
but the thing is, that's
not my target audience.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.290
Statisticians and scholars

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:13.790
I mean, maybe they'll
read the book.

00:11:13.790 --> 00:11:15.707
I'm more than happy for
them to read the book,

00:11:15.707 --> 00:11:17.960
but that's not the audience
that I'm going for.

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:20.080
And by calling it
R without statistics,

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:23.740
I'm intentionally trying to
turn some people on, but also

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:27.520
turn some people off.

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:30.160
Lesson 2: focus on results.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:33.190
So it's really easy
for those of us

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:36.980
who are technical to get really
into the tools that we use.

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:38.980
You know, we want to talk
about this new package

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:41.230
or this new function
that I learn.

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:43.330
For clients, though, what
they really care about

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:45.790
is getting to the goal.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:47.980
As an example of this.

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:50.020
A couple, two or
three years ago,

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:52.870
a potential client came to us
from the state of Connecticut

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:56.380
and they said, David, we
have to produce 169 reports,

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:59.140
one for each town in the
state of Connecticut.

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:00.400
We use Excel.

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:02.150
We have no idea how
this is going to work.

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:04.607
I've heard that R can
maybe do this. In my head

00:12:04.607 --> 00:12:06.190
what I'm thinking
is, "Yeah, of course,

00:12:06.190 --> 00:12:08.080
"like R Markdown,
parameterized reporting,

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:10.530
and pagedown package,"
but that's not at all

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:11.780
what I'm going to say to them.

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:13.572
Instead, I'm going to
frame it as, yeah, we

00:12:13.572 --> 00:12:16.180
can help you to automate this,
we can do it more efficiently,

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:18.940
and we'll also do it in a
way that there will be fewer

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:20.750
copy paste errors.

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:24.520
They care about that end result.
They don't care about the tools

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:26.740
generally that we're using.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:30.040
So we did that report and
this is what we ended up

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:32.920
producing entirely
with the tools

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:36.100
that I talked about. On
the R for the Rest of Us

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:39.730
website on the consulting
page, the headline

00:12:39.730 --> 00:12:43.190
is "Communicate More
Effectively and Efficiently."

00:12:43.190 --> 00:12:47.140
Note that I'm not talking
about any R packages, anything

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:47.930
like that.

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:49.983
I'm talking about how to
communicate effectively

00:12:49.983 --> 00:12:51.400
and efficiently,
which is language

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:53.410
that I think will
resonate with my clients

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:55.340
and potential clients.

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:58.190
Lesson 3 is to make
your client the hero.

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:00.980
It's really easy when you're
developing marketing materials

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:04.730
early on to say, I can
do this, I can do that.

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:07.310
But really, the way you
want to position yourself

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:10.140
is as the guide that
is helping your client.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:15.150
But the client remains
the hero of the story.

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:20.070
So this is a report we
did in 2020, maybe 2021

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:24.190
on census outreach efforts
in the 2020 census.

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:26.190
And when I write about
this, I have a case study

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:28.090
on the R for the
Rest of Us website.

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:29.550
Here's what I say.

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:33.780
The 52 reports helped ORS
Impact and the Democracy

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:36.120
Funders Network, that was
our client, to achieve

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:38.380
their goals and more.

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:42.130
And they did this without
the agonizing manual work.

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:44.170
So I'm talking about--
I'm framing it in a way

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:46.850
that I think will resonate
with potential new clients.

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:50.380
And I'm framing the
client here as the hero,

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:53.210
not saying, you
know, we did this.

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:54.970
I'm saying we
helped them to make

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:58.480
these reports without that
agonizing manual work.

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:01.930
Last lesson is this:
relationships matter

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:03.820
more than technical skills.

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:07.000
When you're starting out, it's
really easy to think, you know,

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:10.125
I just got to tweak this one
bit of CSS on my website.

00:14:10.125 --> 00:14:11.500
To be honest, that
doesn't really

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:13.372
matter that much when
you first start out

00:14:13.372 --> 00:14:15.580
because your first clients
are almost certainly going

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:17.860
to come from people you know.

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:20.050
At the same time that
you cultivate existing

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:22.960
relationships, it's important
to cultivate new relationships.

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:24.910
So for me, what this
often looks like is

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:28.380
I'll see people tweet
about R for the Rest of Us.

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:29.980
And I'm really
grateful when they do.

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:31.800
Of course, it's like
fantastic marketing for me,

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:33.300
so I'll follow up
with them and I'll

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:35.200
say thank you for doing that.

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:36.640
I really appreciate that.

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:39.570
And I'll do something like
give them a free course.

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:42.830
And so by doing that, you
know, I make, hopefully,

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:44.080
cultivate a good relationship.

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:47.040
But then also maybe this person
signs up for another course

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:50.040
or R in 3 Months or
mentions it to a colleague.

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:52.410
I think it can lead to
other things in the future.

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:57.490
So to wrap up, I want to
leave you with this idea.

00:14:57.490 --> 00:14:59.950
You are not me.

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:03.130
I've given examples
here and I've

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:07.270
drawn lessons from the work
of R for the Rest of Us.

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:10.390
But it's really important
to focus not so much

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:13.450
on the specific tactics
that we've used, rather

00:15:13.450 --> 00:15:14.902
focusing on the larger lessons.

00:15:14.902 --> 00:15:16.360
As an example, if
you were thinking

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:18.790
about becoming a consultant,
working with data science

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:22.120
teams, you'd probably talk
more about the technical side

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:24.370
of things than we tend to.

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:26.530
But you still want to
do it in a way that

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:29.200
frames the conversation
around the end result,

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:34.030
as opposed to an overemphasis on
the technical tools themselves.

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:37.900
So I hope I have left
you with the idea

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:43.080
that it is possible to sell your
work without selling your soul.

